WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.480
From a beef between show hosts to a beef between&nbsp;
co-hosts, "Ridiculousness" may have been known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.340
for its wild video clips, but it was nothing&nbsp;
compared to what happened behind the scenes.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.300
At the beginning of every episode, a warning tells&nbsp;
viewers not to attempt any stunts or activities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.940
presented throughout the show. It also condemns&nbsp;
the submission of videos of any kind from viewers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:29.220
If "Ridiculousness" doesn't accept submissions,&nbsp;
then where are they getting their clips? Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.920
the viral videos come from the internet, it sounds&nbsp;
as simple as a Google search, but licensing is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.040
tricky business. In a 2019 interview with Jon&nbsp;
Interviews, host Rob Dyrdek revealed he isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.780
the one searching for the videos but instead has&nbsp;
a massive organization that handles the process.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.280
A team of people scours the internet,&nbsp;
and when the perfect video is found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.320
they try to make contact with the video's&nbsp;
original uploader. Distractify notes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.600
someone on Reddit claimed to receive a one-time&nbsp;
$200 payment for their video. It's unclear if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.800
this is a regular practice for the show,&nbsp;
but with an endless supply of videos online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.220
we don’t have to worry about Rob&nbsp;
running out of hilarious content.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.820
Talk about a major earworm. If you’re not old&nbsp;
enough to know of the hit '80s rock band "Devo",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.780
you might think the catchy theme song&nbsp;
came from "Ridiculousness." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.040
the theme song is actually a cover of&nbsp;
Devo's "Uncontrollable Urge" from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.960
band's classic debut album, "Q:&nbsp;
Are We Not Men? A: We Are Devo!".

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.360
In Season 4, Episode 9 of "Fantasy&nbsp;
Factory," Dyrdek teams up with Devo band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.060
member Mark Mothersbaugh to record the cover for&nbsp;
"Ridiculousness." Mothersbaugh has an expansive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.580
career himself. Along with co-founding the band&nbsp;
"Devo," his solo endeavors have included composing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.680
music for film and TV, such as Nickelodeon's&nbsp;
"Rugrats" and Wes Anderson's features "Bottle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:55.560
Rocket," "Rushmore," "The Royal Tenenbaums,"&nbsp;
and "The Life Aquatic with Steve Zissou." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.700
next time the "Ridiculousness" theme song gets&nbsp;
stuck in your head, you know who to blame.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.520
"Ridiculousness" isn't only big in the&nbsp;
US. The show airs all around the globe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.340
but instead of dubbing episodes, each country&nbsp;
gets its own hosts reacting to clips. In 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.560
"Ridículos Chile" was the first international&nbsp;
version of the show, followed by "Ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.420
Made In France," "Vergüenza Ajena:&nbsp;
Made in Spain," and more in 2016.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.280
The effective format of "Ridiculousness"&nbsp;
has also been used in multiple spin-offs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.700
including MTV's "Amazingness", "Deliciousness,"&nbsp;
"Messyness," and "Adorableness." The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.680
spin-off, "Amazingness," deviated the most&nbsp;
from the "Ridiculousness" format. Hosted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.960
Dyrdek himself, the talent competition&nbsp;
ran from December 2017 to January 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.880
featuring six contestants performing&nbsp;
in front of judges Chris "Drama" Pfaff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:54.360
Eddie Huang, and Krystal Bee. A $10,000 prize&nbsp;
was awarded to the final performer standing.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.180
The other three spin-offs do not feature Dyrdek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:03.120
but they stay true to his style. Tiffani Thiessen&nbsp;
commentates on disastrous food-related clips on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.200
"Deliciousness," James Davis commentates&nbsp;
on the kind of clips that make you cry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.480
out "aww" on "Adorableness," and Snooki&nbsp;
commentates on pure chaos on "Messyness."

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.140
MTV's launch in 1981 changed the course&nbsp;
of television by being the first to air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:25.800
24/7 music-related programming and music videos.&nbsp;
However, with the internet offering a new way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.660
fans to access music content, the network&nbsp;
pivoted to the reality programming genre.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:36.120
This jarring change in content devastated&nbsp;
an entire generation. But on August 1st,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:43.080
2021, MTV's 40th anniversary, the network had the&nbsp;
perfect opportunity to cater to long-time viewers.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.240
When scheduling was announced, Gen X was&nbsp;
disappointed all over again. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.600
the usual 113 hours a week of "Ridiculousness,"&nbsp;
MTV celebrated its birthday by playing the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:00.360
for 24 hours straight. Twitter users and outlets&nbsp;
such as Barstool Sports expressed their outrage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.120
while "Ridiculousness" fans&nbsp;
celebrated in the background.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.460
A hot topic of discussion around&nbsp;
"Ridiculousness" is the long-time feud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:13.680
between Dyrdek and Daniel Tosh of "Tosh.0."&nbsp;
It all started with a tweet from Tosh:

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.820
"Congrats @robdyrdek. new show&nbsp;
looks very funny &amp; familiar."

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.440
Dyrdek tweeted back,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.460
"Thanks you so much @danieltosh!!&nbsp;
Maybe later this week you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.360
@BobSaget and @Tom_Bergeron can&nbsp;
come down to my factory and play."

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.300
In an interview with Larry King, Dyrdek revealed&nbsp;
that both "Ridiculousness" and "Fantasy Factory"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.700
were pitched and sold before "Tosh.0" aired&nbsp;
— but "Fantasy Factory" was produced first.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.060
"By the time my — 'Ridiculousness' — came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.120
it was already after the first&nbsp;
season of Tosh came out, right?"

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.100
It can be argued there are more differences&nbsp;
between the shows than similarities. The comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.960
styles are on opposite sides of the spectrum,&nbsp;
with "Tosh.0" taking a more scripted approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.640
and sprinkling skits into episodes. The&nbsp;
argument between the two came to a head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.660
when Tosh featured a sketch targeting&nbsp;
Dyrdek for copying his show's premise.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.840
In a now-deleted tweet, Dyrdek responded with,

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.160
"I am not a comedian or an actor&nbsp;
so unfortunately for @danieltosh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.740
we are going to have to settle&nbsp;
this the old fashioned way."

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.080
This was followed by Tosh's tweet,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.300
"fair enough but if you have to beat me up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.780
can we film it and put it on my show&nbsp;
so people will actually watch it?"

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.660
Dyrdek's co-hosts, Chanel West Coast&nbsp;
and Steelo Brim, play a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:33.060
role in the show's success. Brim controls the&nbsp;
playback of the clips, slowing down, rewinding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.260
and zooming in on the funniest moments.&nbsp;
And it isn't an episode of "Ridiculousness"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:42.060
without Chanel's contagious laugh. So&nbsp;
how did Dyrdek find his two co-hosts?

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:48.480
Chicago-native Steelo Brim moved to Los Angeles&nbsp;
to pursue a music career. Brim told CBS News&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.160
that he was helping Dyrdek’s cousin start a&nbsp;
publishing and production company when he met Rob.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.500
"Rob just so happened to come&nbsp;
to the studio, like, twice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.620
and was just like, 'Hey man, you’re&nbsp;
funny. We should do a show together.'"

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.960
Dyrdek followed through by bringing on&nbsp;
Brim as co-host of "Ridiculousness." He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.540
also regularly appeared in "Fantasy Factory" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.080
Dyrdek's animation series "Wild&nbsp;
Grinders" as the voice of Meaty.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.140
In an interview on the podcast "No Jumper," Chanel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.780
explained how she managed to&nbsp;
meet Dyrdek through Myspace.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.980
"I would go and I would at all&nbsp;
the top artists on MySpace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.480
and then I would at all their top friends."

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:27.360
One of those artists was Dyrdek's cousin Chris&nbsp;
"Drama" Pfaff. Through a friendship with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.620
Chanel met Dyrdek and hit it off.&nbsp;
The rest, as they say, is history.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.820
Yet another feud sparks between Chanel West&nbsp;
Coast and radio host Charlamagne tha God. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:44.340
Episode 26 of Season 9, Charlamagne appears as&nbsp;
a guest host on the show. During the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.680
Charlamagne says he values being straightforward&nbsp;
with people and not being afraid to convey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.570
his opinions to people's faces. One of those&nbsp;
opinions is about Chanel's rapping abilities.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:57.540
"She’s saying that ‘cause I told her&nbsp;
she’s a wack rapper one time, that’s all."

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.780
Chanel defended herself against the claims.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.020
"I’m just saying, though. You can have opinions,&nbsp;
but it doesn't mean they're right though."

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.780
She then flaunts her long list of accomplishments&nbsp;
as an established artist. Outlets blew the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.760
up, but Chanel admits the beef didn't last&nbsp;
more than five minutes. She tells TooFab,

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.400
"It's not so much that I took it personally, it's&nbsp;
that I knew he was stating I was a wack rapper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.200
on a platform where all my fans were watching.&nbsp;
The last thing I was gonna do was let somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.300
call me a wack rapper and not use that time right&nbsp;
there to state the facts and set things straight."

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.760
Can you think of a better job than getting&nbsp;
to react to funny videos all day long with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.660
your friends? Still, that doesn't take&nbsp;
away from how much hard work it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.160
to film hundreds of episodes over the&nbsp;
course of 28 seasons. What started as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.360
filming a single episode for 12 hours&nbsp;
turned into filming six episodes daily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.040
Brim attributes this change to the hosts&nbsp;
taking on a more improvisational style.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.680
Each episode calls for an outfit change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.000
and the hosts' energy varies depending on&nbsp;
how early or late the production is into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.500
filming. Chanel told In Touch that there's&nbsp;
a reason why she sometimes looks tired.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.840
"If you ever see me looking a little extra&nbsp;
tired, it's probably either the last episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:10.980
of the day, where I was just like, 'forget&nbsp;
the touchup, let's get out of here,' or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.320
it's the first episode of the day&nbsp;
where I was still really tired."

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.740
Dyrdek never wanted to be the old guy on TV. His&nbsp;
one true passion has always been entrepreneurship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:24.780
but it was impossible to pass up the money that&nbsp;
came with his shows on MTV. In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.460
with Graham Bensinger, Dyrdek talked about how&nbsp;
he almost walked away from "Ridiculousness".

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.600
"I don’t wanna be on MTV when I’m, when&nbsp;
I’m 40. I don’t wanna be Kurt Loder."

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.920
It's no secret that money has played a&nbsp;
significant role in Dyrdek's long-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.840
partnership with MTV. So how much does he&nbsp;
make from "Ridiculousness"? In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.100
on Jason Tartick's podcast "Trading&nbsp;
Secrets," Dyrdek revealed the number.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:53.280
"They would only pay me $35,000 an episode to do&nbsp;
'Ridiculousness,' and then they offered me the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.080
$125,000. And then when I got the&nbsp;
integration rights, then I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.260
'I'm just gonna do this because I&nbsp;
can just make millions doing this.’”

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.220
Later on, three shows in with MTV, Dyrdek said,

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:08.160
"I was able to get way more&nbsp;
money based on the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.960
of what had happened with 'Fantasy Factory.'"

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:16.020
Of course, that $125,000 is just the&nbsp;
talent fee. That number doesn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.760
include the money made from integration deals&nbsp;
that Dyrdek took advantage of in all his shows.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:24.660
Dyrdek explained that if MTV&nbsp;
didn’t offer a massive paycheck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:30.000
Season 4 would've ended his hosting gig for&nbsp;
"Ridiculousness." MTV knew the stakes were high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.980
so when it came to negotiations, Dyrdek had&nbsp;
a leg up and got an offer he couldn't refuse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.060
After the signing of the contract,&nbsp;
Dyrdek called MTV a great partner.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.520
As an entrepreneur, Dyrdek didn't just get&nbsp;
lucky. He knew exactly what he was doing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.440
used his platform on MTV as a way to make&nbsp;
millions elsewhere. The entire premise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.060
"Fantasy Factory" was tailored around product&nbsp;
integration. Dydek would write intricate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.360
integrated brand partnerships&nbsp;
with advertisers into the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.640
and then would use those same advertisers to make&nbsp;
other big deals on the side for his own ventures.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.460
Whether he's promoting clothing brands by wearing&nbsp;
them on "Rob &amp; Big" and "Ridiculousness" or doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:13.380
stunts in a Carl's Jr. costume on "Fantasy&nbsp;
Factory," Dyrdek's savvy marketing skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.340
granted him the ability to broaden his&nbsp;
financial reach beyond the MTV network.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:23.460
In addition to a fun, laugh-filled time,&nbsp;
each episode of "Ridiculousness" offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:28.830
a variety of star-studded guest hosts. Some of&nbsp;
the most popular appearances include from Riff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:35.700
Raff and Ray J from Season 3; Kendall and Kylie&nbsp;
Jenner from Season 5; Eric Andre from Seasons 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:41.400
8, and 20; and Dyrdek's frequent collaborator&nbsp;
Christopher "Big Black" Boykin, who tragically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:46.980
passed in 2017. More recent episodes have&nbsp;
seen a decline in celebrity guest hosts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.120
but some of the show's most memorable&nbsp;
moments can be linked to their many guests.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.260
Take, for example, an episode with&nbsp;
Kevin Hart where an entire segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.120
titled "Big Ass Man Pigeons" was inspired&nbsp;
by Hart's fearful encounter with an ostrich.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.580
"He didn't move his body but his head&nbsp;
like spinned all the way around and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.150
started following me, and I was like, 'Yo&nbsp;
man, get the f--- outta here, ostrich.'"

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:13.980
Only "Ridiculousness" can think of a&nbsp;
connection like that. Or Eric Andre's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:18.300
visits, which largely consist of him&nbsp;
flirting with co-host Chanel West Coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:23.520
and an email's worth of swear words that needed&nbsp;
to be censored after taping. One way or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.240
celebrities are always bringing&nbsp;
something special to "Ridiculousness."

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:32.520
Most people aren't familiar with Chanel&nbsp;
West Coast's long-term connection to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:38.100
"Ridiculousness" studio. Chanel has taken many&nbsp;
different avenues in her career, from recording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:44.100
music to acting in MTV's "The Hard Times of RJ&nbsp;
Berger" to becoming a supporting cast member on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:51.000
the reality TV series "Love &amp; Hip Hop: Hollywood."&nbsp;
However, as Chanel told InTouch, a recurring theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.100
in her career has always been the Hollywood Center&nbsp;
Studio, where "Ridiculousness" does its filming.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.840
"There's, like, this weird divine&nbsp;
thing about this studio. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:04.320
my first job was there. Then I ended&nbsp;
up filming 'Ridiculousness' there."

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.940
Maybe we can attribute the great success of&nbsp;
"Ridiculousness" to this magical occurrence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.460
One can only hope Chanel's future endeavors will&nbsp;
have her revisit the studio time and time again.

